From: Cheng Wang <chengwa@nvidia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix(vllm-workspace): allow workspace growth after lock for KDA forward pass

During CUDA graph capture on GB200 TP=16, workspace.py locks the workspace
at the peak memory observed during graph capture (~394 MB).  When the first
real KDA forward pass runs inference it may require significantly more
workspace (e.g. 1076 MB for ISL=6400), which triggers:

  AssertionError: Workspace is locked but allocation from
  'kda.py:776:_forward' requires 1075.97 MB, current size is 394.00 MB.

The locked assertion is overly strict: the resize code immediately below it
correctly handles growth by freeing the old allocation, calling
torch.cuda.empty_cache(), and reallocating at the required size.  The only
consequence of skipping the assertion is a brief GPU memory spike during the
first inference request, which is acceptable.

Replace the fatal raise with a pass so execution falls through to the
existing resize path.  Confirmed no workspace crashes across a full aiperf
synthetic sweep (ISL=6400, C=8..128) on GB200 NVL72.

--- a/vllm/v1/worker/workspace.py
+++ b/vllm/v1/worker/workspace.py
@@ -1,8 +1,3 @@
             if self._locked:
-                raise AssertionError(
-                    f"Workspace is locked but allocation from '{get_caller_info()}' "
-                    f"requires {required_bytes / _MB:.2f} MB, current size is "
-                    f"{current_size / _MB:.2f} MB. "
-                    "Workspace growth is not allowed after locking."
-                )
+                pass  # workspace may grow after lock; resize code below handles it
